d}x}(5{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+2(2x)-0 \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186102306 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102309 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186102324 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102326 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186102330 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102335 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186105610 1074 "The polar equation\n\n$$\nr=2a\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\ndefines a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ centered at the point $(0,a)$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr=4\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nr=4\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\ngives a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\left|2\\right| = 2\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(0,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6a1f74e065f3508c92f8a438eb1830aeb4f877c355cd7779e86183a0da92183.png)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186105610 1074 "The polar equation $r=2a\\sin\\theta$ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=4\\sin\\theta,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=4\\sin\\theta$ gives a circle of radius $\\left|2\\right| = 2$ centered at the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6a1f74e065f3508c92f8a438eb1830aeb4f877c355cd7779e86183a0da92183.png)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186105611 1074 "The diagram shows a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & =\\end{vmatrix}-4.5 &= 4.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(0,-4.5)$.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 2a\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-4.5) \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&=-9\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186105611 1074 "The diagram shows a circle 