ae14028b76.png)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186105613 1074 "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{2})}{(\\frac{7}{2})}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(r, \\theta\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-7\\cos\\theta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8aec18033a85129c44765d2670ebe9c931b0c0186c46b74f9b0ea0ae14028b76.png)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186105669 1074 "The polar equation\n\n$$\nr=2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\ndefines a circle of radius $\\mid a \\mid$ centered at the point $(a,0)$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr=2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{2}{2} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nr=2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\ngives a circle of radius\n\n$$\n\\left|1\\right| = 1\n$$\n\ncentered at the point $(1,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bc5f763522aec68e97bef90126866fe6b2fe7246560d4dc9ddd2c71a5a524d88.png)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186105669 1074 "The polar equation $r=2a\\cos\\theta$ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our cas