 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543033 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186109027 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543093 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186109027 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542796 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110461 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2} \\sin{x} \\cos{x} = -\\sin{x}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi$. If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ are written in ascending order, then what is $x_2\\cdot x_3$?" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110461 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2} \\sin{x} \\cos{x} = -\\sin{x}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi.$ If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ are written in ascending order, then what is $x_2\\cdot x_3?$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110461 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x\\cos x &= -\\sin x \\\\\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x\\cos x + \\sin x &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin x(\\sqrt{2}\\cos x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1 =0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$ are $x = 0,\\, \\pi$.\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1= 0$, or equivalently $\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$. The solution to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$ is $x =\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0,\\quad x_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},\\quad x_3= \\pi\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\cdot \\pi=\\dfrac{3\\pi^2}{4}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110461 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1 =0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$  are $x = 0,\\, \\pi.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1= 0,$ or equivalently $\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ The solution to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$  is $x =\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0,\\quad x_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},\\quad x_3= \\pi.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\cdot  \\pi=\\dfrac{3\\pi^2}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110462 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\sin{\\theta }\\tan{\\theta }-\\sin{\\theta } = 0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ.$ If the solutions $\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\theta_3, \\theta_4$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3?$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110462 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\sin{\\theta}\\tan{\\theta}-\\sin{\\theta} = 0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$. If the solutions $\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\theta_3, \\theta_4$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3$?" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110462 1074 "Note that the function $\\tan{\\theta}$ is not defined at\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nso these values cannot be solutions to the equation.\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3}\\sin θ\\tan θ - \\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin θ(\\sqrt{3}\\tan θ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta} - 1 =0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{\\theta} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$ are $\\theta = 0^\\circ, \\, 180^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta} - 1 =0$, or equivalently $\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta < 360^\\circ$ are $\\theta=30^\\circ, \\, 210^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_2= 30^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_3=180^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_4= 210^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3 =30 \\cdot 180 = 5\\,400\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110462 1074 "Note that the function $\\tan{\\theta}$ is not defined at $\\theta = 90^\\circ , 270^\\circ,$ so these values cannot be solutions to the equation. \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta } = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta } - 1 =0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{\\theta} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ$  are $\\theta = 0^\\circ, \\, 180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta } - 1 =0,$ or equivalently $\\tan{\\theta} =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ$ are $\\theta=30^\\circ, \\, 210^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_2= 30^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_3=180^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_4= 210^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3 =30 \\cdot  180 = 5\\,400.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110463 1071 "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} \\tan{x} = \\tan{x}$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110463 1071 "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} \\tan{x} = \\tan{x}$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110463 1074 "Note that the function $\\csc{x}$ is not defined at the points where\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0\n$$\n\nOn the interval\n\n$$\n0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nthe excluded values are\n\n$$\nx = 0^\\circ, 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\tan{x}$ is not defined at the points where\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = 0\n$$\n\nIn the given interval, the excluded values are\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ, 270^\\circ\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc x\\tan x &= \\tan x \\\\\n\\csc x\\tan x - \\tan x &= 0 \\\\\n(\\csc x - 1)\\tan x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} - 1 = 0, \\qquad \\tan{x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\csc{x} -1= 0$, or equivalently, $\\csc x=1$. The solution to this equation for $0 ^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ is $x = 90^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\tan{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ\\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ are $x = 0^\\circ,\\, 180^\\circ$.\n\nFinally, taking into account the values excluded from the interval, we conclude that the given equation has no solution." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110463 1074 "Note that the function $\\csc{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\sin{x} = 0.$ On the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ,$ the excluded values are $x = 0^\\circ , 180^\\circ.$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\tan{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x} = 0.$ In the given interval, the excluded values are $x = 90^\\circ , 270^\\circ.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\csc{x} - 1 = 0, \\qquad \\tan{x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\csc{x} -1= 0,$ or equivalently, $\\csc x=1.$ The solution to this equation for $0 ^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ is $x = 90^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\tan{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ\\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$  are $x = 0^\\circ,\\, 180^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, taking into account the values excluded from the interval, we conclude that the given equation has no solution." 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110464 1071 "The graph shows the curve $y = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$ and the curve $y = \\cos{x}$ on the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$. The curves intersect at the points $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$, as shown. Find $x$-coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/d3/d3462ebb123dcaf59391787ffae5b39dea5809eefe656cfda2f17498259dc1b6.png)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110464 1071 "The graph shows the curve $y = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$ and the curve $y = \\cos{x}$ on the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$ The curves intersect at the points $A,$ $B,$ $C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Find $x$-coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/d3/d3462ebb123dcaf59391787ffae5b39dea5809eefe656cfda2f17498259dc1b6.png)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110464 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives\nthe equation\n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x\\sin x - \\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\cos x(2\\sin x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = 0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\cos{x} = 0$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x= 90^\\circ, \\, 270^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0$, or equivalently $\\sin{x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$. The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x = 30^\\circ,\\, 150^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ, \\quad x_2=90^\\circ,\\quad x_3=150^\\circ, \\quad x_4 = 270^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110464 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives \nthe equation\n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}  = 0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\cos{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x= 90^\\circ, \\, 270^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} - 1  = 0,$ or equivalently $\\sin{x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x = 30^\\circ,\\, 150^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ, \\quad x_2=90^\\circ,\\quad x_3=150^\\circ, \\quad x_4 = 270^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110490 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(2)-{log}_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110490 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} (\\frac{2}{5}) &= \\log_{3} (2) - \\log_{3} (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110491 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(16)-{log}_{5}⁡(8) & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{16}{8}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110491 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{5} (16) - \\log_{5} (8) &= \\log_{5} (\\frac{16}{8}) \\\\\n&= \\log_{5} (2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110492 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{2}⁡(25)-2{log}_{2}⁡(5) & =2({log}_{2}⁡(25)-{log}_{2}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =2({log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{25}{5})) \\\\  & =2{log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\log_2(25) - 2\\log_2(5) = 2\\log_2(5).\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110492 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(a) - \\log_n(b) = \\log_n\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\log_{2} (25) - 2\\log_{2} (5) &= 2(\\log_{2} (25) - \\log_{2} (5)) \\\\\n&= 2(\\log_{2} (\\frac{25}{5})) \\\\\n&= 2\\log_{2} (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\log_2(25) - 2\\log_2(5) = 2\\log_2(5)\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110493 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln(a) - \\ln(b) = \\ln\\left( \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{p}{q}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(q) \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110493 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln(a) - \\ln(b) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (\\frac{p}{q}) &= \\ln (p) - \\ln (q) \\\\\n&= 6 - 4 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110497 1071 "By expressing $\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)$ in the form $k\\ln x$, determine the value of $k$." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110497 1071 "By expressing $\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)$ in the form $k\\ln x,$ determine the value of $k.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110497 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k =2.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110497 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left(c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (x^{2}) &= 2\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k =2$." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110498 1071 "By expressing $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_3{x}$ in the form $\\log_3 \\left(x^k\\right)$, determine the value of $k$." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110498 1071 "By expressing $\\dfrac{1}{2}\\log_3{x}$ in the form $\\log_3 \\left(x^k\\right),$ determine the value of $k.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110498 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left( c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡x & ={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110498 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n(c^m)= m\\log_n\\left(c \\right)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{3} x &= \\log_{3} (x^{1/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110499 1071 "Find the value of $\\ln\\left(x^3\\right)$ if $\\ln(x) = 0.2$." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110499 1071 "Find the value of $\\ln\\left(x^3\\right)$ if $\\ln(x) = 0.2.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110499 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left( c^m \\right) = m\\ln{c}\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}) & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110499 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln\\left(c^m \\right) = m\\ln{c}\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms and the information given, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (x^{3}) &= 3 \\cdot \\ln (x) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 0.2 \\\\\n&= 0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186110589 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543483 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110590 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543479 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543514 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110592 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543532 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110592 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543547 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110596 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543525 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110596 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543528 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110597 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543537 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110597 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543549 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110598 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543472 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110598 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543545 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543500 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543506 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543522 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543582 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543590 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543470 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543499 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543567 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543568 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543513 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543538 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543559 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543592 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543484 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543542 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110840 1050 1500.0 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110843 1050 420.0 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186110844 1050 420.0 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111010 1071 "Consider the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111010 1071 "Consider the equation $2\\sin x\\cos x =-\\sin x$ for $- π \\le x < π$. If the solutions $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111010 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $  are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $  are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111055 1071 "Express $\\log_{2} (x^{3}) - \\log_{2} (x)$ as a single logarithm." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111055 1071 "Express ${log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3})-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$ as a single logarithm." 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111055 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3})-{log}_{2}⁡(x) & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111147 1071 "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sin x\\sec x - \\sec x = 0$ for $0^{∘} \\le x < 360^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111147 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111147 1074 "Note that the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ the excluded values are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0.$ Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ so it is never equal to zero. Therefore, this equation has no solutions.\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={30}^{∘},\\,{150}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\,500.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111212 1071 "By expressing $\\frac{5}{2}\\log_{2} t$ in the form $\\log_{2} (t^{k})$, determine the value of $k$." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111212 1071 "By expressing $\\frac{5}{2}{log}_{2}⁡t$ in the form ${log}_{2}⁡({t}^{k}),$ determine the value of $k.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111212 1074 "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{log}_{2}⁡t & ={log}_{2}⁡({t}^{5/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $k=\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111217 1071 "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8})=$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111217 1071 "What is $\\ln (\\frac{e^{3}}{8})$?" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111217 1074 "First, let's recall the quotient rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for natural logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\  & =3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111868 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0,  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0.$ The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0,$ or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12.$ The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111868 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x\\cos x - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin x &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin x(\\cos x - \\frac{1}{2}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0$. The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0$, or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12$. The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111869 1064 "We can solve some trigonometric equations containing reciprocal functions using the zero product rule.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n \n\n$$\n(\\sec x -1)(2\\sin x +1)= 0, \\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the function $\\sec{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x}=0.$ On the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ the excluded values are $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nThe equation is already factored, so by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} -1 =0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sec{x} -1 =0,$ or equivalently, $\\sec{x} =1.$ The only solution to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ is $x = 0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0,$ or equivalently, $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac12.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ are $x=-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions to the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 =  - \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\quad x_2 = - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\quad x_3 = 0.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111869 1064 "We can solve some trigonometric equations containing reciprocal functions using the zero product rule.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n(\\sec x -1)(2\\sin x +1)= 0, \\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the function $\\sec{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x}=0$. On the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$, the excluded values are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nThe equation is already factored, so by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} -1 =0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sec{x} -1 =0$, or equivalently, $\\sec{x} =1$. The only solution to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ is $x = 0$.\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0$, or equivalently, $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac12$. The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ are $x=-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions to the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\quad x_2 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\quad x_3 = 0\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111878 1064 "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**.\n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum.\n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} 4 - \\log_{3} (6) &= \\log_{3} (\\frac{4}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (\\frac{2}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186111878 1064 "Suppose we want to rewrite $\\log_3(4) - \\log_3(6)$ as a single logarithm. We can do this using the **quotient rule**. \n\nThe quotient rule is very similar to the product rule, except that this time we're dealing with the difference of two logarithms rather than the sum. \n\nThe quotient rule states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n} (a) - \\log_{\\color{blue}n} (b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left(\\dfrac a b\\right) .\n$$\n\nSo, we can use this rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}3}(4) - \\log_{\\color{blue}3}(6)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(4)-{log}_{3}⁡(6) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{4}{6}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111880 1064 "The **power rule** allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n\n$$\n\\log_{n}\\left( c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"\n\nFor example, let's use the rule to simplify $\\log\\left(125\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(125) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(125) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule is just a shortcut for the above procedure." 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186111880 1064 "The **power rule** allows us to simplify logarithms of exponentiated expressions. It states that\n\n$$\n\\log_{n}\\left(c^{\\color{blue}m} \\right) = {\\color{blue}m}\\log_{n}{c}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the logarithm has the effect of \"bringing down the power.\"\n\nFor example, let's use the rule to simplify\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(125\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (125) &= \\log (5^{3}) \\\\\n&= 3\\log (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why the power rule works, notice that we can reach the same result using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log (125) &= \\log (5^{3}) \\\\\n&= \\log (5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5) \\\\\n&= \\log (5) + \\log (5) + \\log (5) \\\\\n