ulate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186152712 1071 "Let $R=[2,4]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{2,3,4\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186152712 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/e8/e88b9d4b756f76a8b7c75287f0c4e3ff07f7b458dcdcea703769fc6da56f2a12.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc9b8c4634691d17614c39e542488fb17d2918737be0dad5d262cd9a1fd827a.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/be/beb238668c9c1ebb66f8c7ec3cd8a40b267b47d7ba1cbd2e351c65b3c17d7a84.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(3,3)=18 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(4,3)=25 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(3,4)=25 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(4,4)=32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =18+25+25+32 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186152713 1071 "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a r