ons, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4\\cdot {M}_{11}+4\\cdot {M}_{21}+4\\cdot {M}_{12}+4\\cdot {M}_{22} \\\\  & =4({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-2x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=6-2x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/77/770593c18eaaebff55c73151b0e98628bd4ece00b75380b29fde8d1721f1cf16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc8062cb5c436a0135abd2340fcaa39560658c4a23f13f0cb70893789a81836.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-2,-2)=12 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(0,-2)=8 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-2,0)=10 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(0,0)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =4(12+8+10+6) \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186153417 1064 "In this lesson, we will describe a process for calculating an **upper bound** for the volume enclosed between a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane. \n\nAs an example, consider the rectangle $R$ in the $xy$-plane, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf7ef4752b0910b5544707eef97c854d8eeeedbc221a81c5fa631cf38b8b8d9a.png)\n\nNow consider the function $f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over $R,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34f7936b29e6a177ae0c831e36654dc5059514580a1947324a40d52320b83e5a.png)\n\nThe space underneath the surface and above the region $R$ defines a fixed volume $V.$ Our goal is to find an upper bound for $V.$ We do this by approximating this volume using rectangular solids. \n\nWe start by partitioning the $x$- and $y$-intervals of $R.$ Let's pick two subintervals of equal length for both $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/c3/c375b605ca6b7d7f2935218976f5e09db5ed2cfb64d811b1fe0a854d02646a36.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is now partitioned into $4$ rectangles:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nWe seek an *upper* bound for the volume $V.$ So, we let $M_{ij}$ be the *largest* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We then define a rectangular solid in each subregion whose base is $R_{ij}$ and whose height is $M_{ij}.$ \n\nFor example, the rectangular solid corresponding to $R_{11}$ is given below. \n\n![](images/62/62cee54b71235dc75cab863f0971735ec221fc61a022edf78994ea3f935c1cd4.png)\n\nThe volume of this rectangular solid is\n\n$$\nM_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11})  = \\dfrac14M_{11}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the volumes of the rectangular solids formed by the partitions $R_{21}, R_{12},$ and $R_{22}$ respectively are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14M_{21}, \\qquad \\dfrac14M_{12}, \\qquad \\dfrac14M_{22}.\n$$\n\nDrawing all four rectangular solids, we get a volume that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f8f44d8a180838cb2b79b775483260ea9a30608cb8a618eaf86a5edcabd2c610.png)\n\nThe upper bound $U$ for our volume is the sum of the volumes of our four rectangular solids. This gives\n\n$$\nU = \\dfrac14(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}).\n$$\n\nThe number $U$ is called the **upper Riemann sum with respect to the given partition**.\n\n**Note:** We don't yet know the values of $M_{ij},$ the height of each solid. We'll learn to figure these out a little later in the lesson. For now, we will just focus on writing $U$ as an expression of $M_{ij}.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186153417 1064 "In this lesson, we will describe a process for calculating an **upper bound** for the volume enclosed between a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane. \n\nAs an example, consider the rectangle $R$ in the $xy$-plane, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf7ef4752b0910b5544707eef97c854d8eeeedbc221a81c5fa631cf38b8b8d9a.png)\n\nNow consider the function $f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over $R,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34f7936b29e6a177ae0c831e36654dc5059514580a1947324a40d52320b83e5a.png)\n\nThe space underneath the surface and above the region $R$ defines a fixed volume $V.$ Our goal is to find an upper bound for $V.$ We do this by approximating this volume using rectangular solids. \n\nWe start by partitioning the $x$- and $y$-intervals of $R.$ Let's pick two subintervals of equal length for both $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/c3/c375b605ca6b7d7f2935218976f5e09db5ed2cfb64d811b1fe0a854d02646a36.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is now partitioned into $4$ rectangles:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nWe seek an *upper* bound for the volume $V.$ So, we let $M_{ij}$ be the *largest* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We then define a rectangular solid in each subregion whose base is $R_{ij}$ and whose height is $M_{ij}.$ \n\nFor example, the rectangular solid corresponding to $R_{11}$ is given below. \n\n![](images/62/62cee54b71235dc75cab863f0971735ec221fc61a022edf78994ea3f935c1cd4.png)\n\nThe volume of this rectangular solid is\n\n$$\nM_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11})  = \\dfrac14M_{11}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the volumes of the rectangular solids formed by the partitions $R_{21}, R_{12},$ and $R_{22}$ respectively are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14M_{21}, \\qquad \\dfrac14M_{12}, \\qquad \\dfrac14M_{22}.\n$$\n\nDrawing all four rectangular solids, we get a volume that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f8f44d8a180838cb2b79b775483260ea9a30608cb8a618eaf86a5edcabd2c610.png)\n\nThe upper bound $U$ for our volume is the sum of the volumes of our four rectangular solids. This gives\n\n$$\nU = \\dfrac14(M_{11} + M_{21} + M_{12} + M_{22}).\n$$\n\nThe number $U$ is called the **upper Riemann sum with respect to the given partition**.\n\n**Note:** We don't yet know the values of $M_{ij},$ the height of each solid. We'll learn to figure these out a little later in the lesson. For now, we will just focus on writing $U$ as an expression of $M_{ij}.$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186161276 1078 #edb/ref 17592186161278 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186162016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543517 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186162664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186162664 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186162664 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}v_{11} & v_{12} \\\\ v_{21} & v_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, what is the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}v_{12} & + & v_{22}\\end{vmatrix}$?" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186162664 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-4I=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186163246 1064 "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n![](images/ab/abe87fb2a304ff102bb3f703d025c7d41f56cb6fab7fc477b3073cbd820cda6e.svg)\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =[\\begin{aligned}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/41/4195807c3f61cd730c57340666c8d651e94d2b277f9623da7cd482c1d7123303.svg)\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/64/64aa31f039ec188355522cffa8a6c62df8c53bb409e55df32ce880d9a9efcd6e.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab0a5a5a284c962414158e5b7c463264b271bdd57decd8e08305285f7243f7bd.svg)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186163246 1064 "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n![](images/ab/abe87fb2a304ff102bb3f703d025c7d41f56cb6fab7fc477b3073cbd820cda6e.svg)\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =\\begin{bmatrix}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/41/4195807c3f61cd730c57340666c8d651e94d2b277f9623da7cd482c1d7123303.svg)\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/64/64aa31f039ec188355522cffa8a6c62df8c53bb409e55df32ce880d9a9efcd6e.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab0a5a5a284c962414158e5b7c463264b271bdd57decd8e08305285f7243f7bd.svg)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186163247 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition. \n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$\nA^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/b7/b766ec7d8c00dfc121e707566d21db470c087c3e785f4e1b2868e7f932873c67.svg)\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving the characteristic equation $|A^T \\! A - \\lambda I| = 0,$ we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad  {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding singular values are \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}  = 4},  \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A.$ These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/f0/f074943374fd488663eda967ab64f335047f9b6cc91c98c504dcb0f2ee8282a6.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/03/03d6a116d751736f50c366cd4721ee7d1dbf39048092272acc6577934f4600c7.svg)\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/16/16813fa19b3417b6de49f9c3c1c27cfedca850b473da01d3b0e0a0b3091b6e28.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/32/32b3c9740740767d4d849ac2d73552dee1e225cc507e95a17c727e8c3fcc6275.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/85/85c926521f0588117992c019ab0db3723520a14ba4c2879d630ab391462b4af2.svg)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186163247 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition. \n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$\nA^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/b7/b766ec7d8c00dfc121e707566d21db470c087c3e785f4e1b2868e7f932873c67.svg)\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving the characteristic equation $|A^T \\! A - \\lambda I| = 0,$ we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad  {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding singular values are \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}  = 4},  \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A.$ These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/f0/f074943374fd488663eda967ab64f335047f9b6cc91c98c504dcb0f2ee8282a6.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/03/03d6a116d751736f50c366cd4721ee7d1dbf39048092272acc6577934f4600c7.svg)\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/16/16813fa19b3417b6de49f9c3c1c27cfedca850b473da01d3b0e0a0b3091b6e28.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/32/32b3c9740740767d4d849ac2d73552dee1e225cc507e95a17c727e8c3fcc6275.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/85/85c926521f0588117992c019ab0db3723520a14ba4c2879d630ab391462b4af2.svg)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186163317 1064 "Consider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61ff6d3a83c2a660ca8369d96ab7bede86f9a124727db8d5a7e320dcd5aaad74.svg)\n\nSuppose we're given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's use this information to find the matrix $U.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=1.$ \n\nEarlier, we saw that the columns of $\\mathbf u$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. Therefore, we compute the columns of $\\mathbf u$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the matrix $U$ in our singular value decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n![](images/15/159ace613dacf666b32131543c77b3eb602e16226ba2d37e9e8bea4dcff1cb56.svg)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186163317 1064 "Consider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61ff6d3a83c2a660ca8369d96ab7bede86f9a124727db8d5a7e320dcd5aaad74.svg)\n\nSuppose we're given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's use this information to find the matrix $U.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=1.$ \n\nEarlier, we saw that the columns of $\\mathbf u$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. Therefore, we compute the columns of $\\mathbf u$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the matrix $U$ in our singular value decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n![](images/15/159ace613dacf666b32131543c77b3eb602e16226ba2d37e9e8bea4dcff1cb56.svg)" 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186166746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543478 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543557 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543551 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543565 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543572 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543471 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543492 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543493 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186166940 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543510 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164741 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164746 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164751 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164756 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186176033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543550 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186176033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543562 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186176033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543587 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183734 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183735 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183736 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183737 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186183738 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542641 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186542946 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543038 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543173 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186183733 1075 #edb/ref 17592186543265 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186183733 1078 #edb/ref 17592186542946 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543482 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543511 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543578 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184342 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543575 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184342 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543586 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184854 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186184854 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$, where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186184854 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 25\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 7+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{\\sqrt{75}\\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot 3}\\sqrt{25\\cdot 2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}})≈{35.26}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186185322 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/a9/a9edbe1fee59758c9557d95ea8f62468f9f3cb98449c437a274bdbf5c30f7dc6.svg)\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$  must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186185322 1064 "Recall that the singular values of a real matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nAlso, since $A^T \\! A$ is a positive semi-definite matrix, the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A$ must be always non-negative real numbers.\n\nLet's compute the singular values of the matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/a9/a9edbe1fee59758c9557d95ea8f62468f9f3cb98449c437a274bdbf5c30f7dc6.svg)\n\nNow, since\n\n- $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix, and\n\n- $A^T$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\nthen $A^T \\! A$  must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 3}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 3} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nLet's now compute our singular values. First, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When listing the singular values of a matrix, we usually write them in descending order.\n\n- If $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, then $A^T A$ is a $n\\times n$ positive semi-definite matrix. This means that $A$ has *at most* $n$ distinct singular values." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186191086 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191089 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186192686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543496 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186192686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543505 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186192686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543544 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186192798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543530 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186193200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186193200 1071 "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, X_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Exp(θ)$ and unknown rate parameter $θ$. The log-likelihood function of the sample $5.1, 4.2, 6.3$ is\n$l(θ) = a\\ln θ + bθ$.\nWhat is the value of $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535925 17592186045417 false] [17592186193200 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $5.1,\\,4.2,\\,6.3$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186193200 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =5.1+4.2+6.3=15.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 15.6 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-15.6)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=3+(-15.6)=-12.6.\n$$" 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186203523 1078 #edb/ref 17592186203524 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186203526 1078 #edb/ref 17592186203527 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543481 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204106 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543515 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543563 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543326 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543333 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543386 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543388 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543411 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543412 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543449 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535925 17592186045417 true] [17592186204604 1071 "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\cdot 3$ matrix\n\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}5.711 & 3.866 & -3.400 \\\\ 3.580 & -0.216 & -3.789 \\\\ -0.507 & -5.693 & -3.727 \\\\ 1.640 & -0.269 & 0.705 \\\\ -0.225 & -4.111 & -2.130\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal compone